WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.400
Well, everyone, it’s happening, we’re
coming up on the 20th anniversary of Peter

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.730
Jackson’s adaptation of J. R. R. Tolkien’s
The Lord of the Rings,

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:11.720
which means it’s the season for comprehensive
retrospectives on all things Tolkien.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.820
Jackson’s adaptation is an undeniable triumph:
both a lushly realized, cohesive vision of

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:20.609
the text, and a masterpiece of savvy fandom
marketing.

00:00:20.609 --> 00:00:24.289
Jackson made extremely effective use of the
internet during production,

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:30.419
curating an official fan club that lent the
production a sense of authority and consensus.

00:00:30.419 --> 00:00:35.300
Whatever disagreements over adaptational decisions,
the boxed set Extended Edition DVDs still

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.859
stand as a masterpiece of filmmaking and an
undeniable set of bonafides,

00:00:39.859 --> 00:00:45.449
the display of a passion project wrought on
a scale that comes along once in a lifetime,

00:00:45.449 --> 00:00:51.250
with dozens of hours of documentary combing
over every minute detail of the process and

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.739
effort that went into lovingly rendering the
world.

00:00:54.739 --> 00:00:59.609
But, before we all get swept away in that
fervor, like Nazgul in the river, I wanted

00:00:59.609 --> 00:01:07.950
to turn some attention towards Ralph Bakshi’s
1978 adaptation.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:12.460
[Swelling Music]

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:20.960
Now, I’m not setting out here to pit these
adaptations against each other, but comparison

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.850
is unavoidable.
The Jackson trilogy looms large in culture,

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:28.600
monolithic even.
Not only is it the first version of the story

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:33.190
an entire generation was exposed to,
it has effectively mediated an agreed-upon

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:37.650
interpretation of the text by virtue of its
success as an adaptation,

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:42.850
by virtue of looking and feeling “right”
to the audience’s eye,

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.880
whatever that nebulous word means in this
context.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.060
Bakshi’s version is undeniably far more
fraught.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:54.549
It is less cohesive, more reliant on an existing
familiarity with the source material,

00:01:54.549 --> 00:02:00.430
frustratingly paced, and ultimately incomplete.
It is often more accurate, more strictly faithful

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:03.680
to the text,
but just as often more hollow,

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.399
with details that are true to the literal
words on the page while missing the underlying

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:12.690
point of those words.
But it’s not without its merits.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:17.110
Many of the adaptation decisions are interesting
or excellent in their own right,

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:21.930
presenting an interpretation of the text that
is wildly different, but just as compelling

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:22.930
as Jackson’s.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:28.530
It is technologically decades ahead of itself,
biting off far more than it can chew in pursuit

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:32.820
of technological solutions that are now standard
practice.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.400
This is a source of a lot of the film’s
inconsistency, but it’s definitely a fascinating

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:38.890
inconsistency.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:43.500
There is, all-in-all a lot more to it than
just being a weird early crack at a fantasy

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.040
epic,
and I think that’s worth talking about.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:50.239
So let me tell you the story of a coked-out
pervert from Brooklyn and the movie he so

00:02:50.239 --> 00:03:06.970
desperately wanted to make.
[Bombastic orchestral music]

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:11.580
First of all, The Lord of the Rings, the book,
was not a runaway hit in the United States.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:15.560
While the component novels were published
in the US within six months of their respective

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.380
UK releases,
the publisher, Houghton Mifflin, under-estimated

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:23.400
how popular the books would be.
The Hobbit had been successful, but that was

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.830
a children’s book released almost twenty
years earlier.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:31.860
A lot had changed in America since 1937,
and so the initial print run of Fellowship

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.159
was only 1500 copies.

00:03:34.159 --> 00:03:40.819
Now, if you only print 1500 copies it’s
hard to sell more than 1500 copies.

00:03:40.819 --> 00:03:45.569
So even though that print run did sell out
it’s not a clear indicator of the actual

00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:49.090
audience for the book
and doesn’t really give you a good idea

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:53.769
of how many you should have printed.
Consequently it took years for sales to ramp

00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:58.310
up as Houghton Mifflin opted to trickle import
copies from the UK instead of issuing new

00:03:58.310 --> 00:03:59.579
print runs.

00:03:59.579 --> 00:04:04.069
Somewhere in the early 1960s this triggered
a protectionist policy in US copyright law

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:09.189
that, at the time,
mandated a domestic manufacturing quota.

00:04:09.189 --> 00:04:12.189
Failing to meet the quota, The Hobbit and
The Lord of the Rings fell into the public

00:04:12.189 --> 00:04:16.700
domain in the United States
where they sat until a law was passed in 1994

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:20.070
to restore the copyright to a number of similarly
odd cases

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:24.830
where otherwise still-copyrighted foreign
works had fallen into public domain in the

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:28.420
US
as a consequence of noncompliance with formalities

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:30.170
like manufacturing requirements.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:34.730
But, point is, even though sales of the books
had picked up dramatically over the course

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:38.170
of the 60s,
particularly gaining traction with the counterculture

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:42.530
crowd who identified with Tolkien’s pastoral
environmentalism,

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:49.130
many of these publications, like the 150,000
copy Ace Books paperback printing in 1965,

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:53.280
weren’t licensed and paid no royalties back
to Tolkien.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.610
This sparked a publicity battle over the issue,
with Tolkien working with Ballantine books

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:02.010
to produce an authoritative authorized paperback
edition,

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:08.100
but is also when the books finally surge in
popularity, a decade after their initial publication.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:13.540
By the 1970s the Tolkien estate was actively
courting adaptations of JRR’s work,

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:17.040
since a licensed adaptation would actually
pay royalties,

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:22.540
and interest was finally there.
In 1969 United Artists purchased the international

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:25.170
film rights to Lord of the Rings directly
from Tolkien

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:29.290
and they started trying to get a viable script
and interested director.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:33.370
The idea of a film version of Lord of the
Rings had already been floating around for

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:36.650
years,
with various speculative projects dating back

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:39.540
to the 50s,
but while the counterculture crowd that had

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:43.680
latched on to the books was increasingly proving
to be a viable economic bloc,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.890
it was still not quite mainstream popular,
which limited the possible budget of any project.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:53.800
United Artists didn’t have much luck,
their attempts to get a full Lord of the Rings

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:59.910
project rolling largely fell apart as the
books developed a reputation of being “unfilmable,”

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:04.090
not because it was impossible,
but because the budget would never be there

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:08.870
to do justice to the book’s many grand locations
and fanciful sets.

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:13.630
The story was simply too long to realistically
compress into a single film,

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:18.110
but setting out to make multiple films without
assured success was folly,

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:22.100
and surely audiences would revolt over an
incomplete story.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:25.810
Any attempt at the time would be too compromised
to satisfy the book’s fans,

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:28.580
and too cheap looking to satisfy anyone else.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:31.140
Then along comes an animator named Ralph Bakshi.

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:37.310
Born in Palestine, but raised in Brooklyn,
Ralph Bakshi is, ya know, a bit of a character.

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:42.550
Everyone else is behind. I’m not ahead.
I’m doing what’s right for an artist who’s

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:47.130
doing what he believes in. I'm not ahead of
my times. What I am is honest. What they are

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:48.130
is dishonest.

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:53.160
He cut his teeth as an animator in the 1950s
and 60s at Terrytoons and Paramount working

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:58.010
on television shows like Mighty Mouse, Deputy
Dawg, and Spider-Man before pivoting to feature

00:06:58.010 --> 00:06:59.650
films in the 1970s.

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:03.510
The Lord of the Rings was Bakshi’s fifth
feature film as a director and it is notable

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:07.690
as both a culmination of his technical interests
as an animator,

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:11.530
utilizing mixed media as both a cost saving
and aesthetic tool,

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:15.150
and the ways in which it deviates from Bakshi’s
normal narrative style.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:18.890
Bakshi was raised in the densely urban Brooklyn
neighbourhood of Brownsville,

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:24.710
a historically poor neighbourhood that while
originally dominated by Jewish factory workers

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:29.130
saw a heavy influx of Black residents through
the 40s, 50s, and 60s.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:33.690
This multiracial urban milieu forms the foundation
of his early theatrical work,

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:37.990
his first three films are all considered part
of the urban street film genre,

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:41.340
but, before that,
alright, to put this career in context we

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.250
need to back up to the sixties again.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:46.690
The sixties were not kind to theatrical animation
in America.

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:50.840
Owing to shifts in the way that movies were
exhibited, starting in the 50s,

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:55.740
shorts became less financially viable, and
so most of the money for animation shifted

00:07:55.740 --> 00:08:01.590
to the rapidly expanding market of television,
which Hanna-Barbera and Warner Brothers dominated.

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:07.620
Theatrical animation was largely the domain
of Walt Disney, both the company and the man.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:11.560
Walt had been an absolutely titanic figure
in the medium for decades,

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:16.110
but on that front things were not great: his
health was declining,

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:21.430
and the output of the studio dropped precipitously
leading up to his death in 1966.

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:25.660
While the studio had managed to put out a
new animated feature approximately every fifteen

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:28.670
months on average over the course of the 40s
and 50s,

00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:32.520
they only managed to complete three films
in the 60s:

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:36.460
One Hundred and One Dalmatians, The Sword
in the Stone, and The Jungle Book,

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:41.770
the animation studio relying mostly on reissues
of their back catalogue for the decade.

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:46.020
While these films performed commercially well,
The Jungle Book in particular being a huge

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:49.560
hit releasing in 1967 as Walt Disney’s final
film,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:55.030
they were not particularly challenging.
These were interwoven with re-issues of Bambi,

00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:58.910
Pinocchio, Cinderella, Snow White, Fantasia,
and Peter Pan.

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:03.940
And the follow up, the first feature after
Walt’s death, is 1970’s The Artistocats,

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:10.050
which is just the absolute pinnacle of Disney’s
historical reputation for anodyne garbage.

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:13.950
So this is what feature animation looks like
if you’re a twenty-something in 1970, this

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:17.600
is what you’ve grown up with.
There’s other stuff, of course, there’s

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:22.450
always something on the periphery,
but overwhelmingly the legacy of theatrical

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:28.760
animation is safe, bright, unscary, trapped
decades in the past, and slowly dying.

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:34.040
In 1968 Ralph Bakshi, moving into his 30s
and frustrated with the stagnant status quo

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:37.640
of the industry,
broke away and formed his own animation studio

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:40.910
in Brooklyn.
Initially the studio found work on shows like

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:45.420
Rocket Robin Hood and Spider-Man,
but Bakshi had ambitions to move into feature

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:48.710
films.
He had a number of projects already in mind,

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:52.390
including The Lord of the Rings which he had
fallen in love with after the books really

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:55.980
broke out in the 60s.
While he would spend years working on a half

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:59.770
dozen projects in parallel,
particularly trying to get in good graces

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:02.810
with United Artists who held the film rights
for Lord of the Rings,

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:07.750
the first film he was able to secure full
funding for was an adaptation of comic artist

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:11.090
Robert Crumb’s underground hit Fritz the
Cat.

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:16.520
Released in 1972 with an X rating the film
was perverted, juvenile, rambling, gratuitously

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:21.820
violent, unfocused, aggressively political,
and a huge success.

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:26.910
Despite the rating limiting distribution options,
the spectacle of a cartoon that was the opposite

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:31.910
of all things Disney drew in a worldwide audience
to the tune of $90 million dollars against

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:36.680
a budget of somewhere between $700,000 and
$1.3 million.

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:40.400
Following the success of Fritz the Cat Bakshi
was able to fund and distribute the animated

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:46.020
quasi-autobiography, pseudo-crime film Heavy
Traffic, released in 1973.

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:50.430
While not the astronomic success of Fritz,
Heavy Traffic made decent money against its

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:54.680
comparably slim budget and is considered a
box office success.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:58.320
Both of these films did well with critics
and remain artistically relevant.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:02.800
Personally I think Heavy Traffic is the better
of the two, and certainly Bakshi’s best

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.370
film from the era,
though it does encapsulate Bakshi’s overall

00:11:06.370 --> 00:11:08.840
sensibilities as a creative.

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:13.790
There is a fixation on Black culture, the
complicated racial identity of being Jewish

00:11:13.790 --> 00:11:17.860
in America,
a deep and total distrust of police, disillusionment

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:22.780
with the results of the counterculture movement,
and a keen sense of the ways the structure

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:27.190
of society is arranged to maintain an underclass.

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:31.860
Artistically there is a fascination with the
idea of capturing reality.

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:36.210
Photographs of real locations are used as
trace references for backgrounds in Fritz,

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:40.540
and many backgrounds in Heavy Traffic are
just stylized photographs.

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:44.460
Both films use some documentary recording
for secondary dialogue,

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:48.970
captured by Bakshi while walking around Harlem
and Brooklyn or interviewing people he met

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:50.860
on the streets or in bars.

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:52.930
&gt;&gt; Man 1: Look, I’m paying my taxes.
&gt;&gt; Woman: The money is what’s happening.

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:53.930
&gt;&gt; Man 1: Hey!
&gt;&gt; Woman: See what I mean? See what I mean?

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:54.930
&gt;&gt; Man 1: No, what I’m talking about
&gt;&gt; Woman: It all counts, that is what’s

00:11:54.930 --> 00:12:01.020
happening, I’m talking about as far as,
like, if you wanna be revolutionary you get

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:07.510
some bread first, and then you can talk trash.
&gt;&gt; Man 2: Whitey blind us with religion.

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:12.740
There’s also a pervasive horniness as it’s
rare to go an entire scene with a woman without

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:17.220
a breast popping out for no reason,
the slapstick humour of Terrytoons is often

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:22.070
extended into bloody hyperviolence,
and there is a complicated relationship with

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:25.870
queer characters.
While Bakshi’s eye as a director is certainly

00:12:25.870 --> 00:12:29.470
sympathetic to drag queens, trans women, and
gay men,

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:34.210
they clearly form an integral part of the
real spaces and communities that he tries

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:39.310
to simulate in his art,
narratively they tend to be present just long

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:44.510
enough to be physically brutalized as a condemnation
of police and bigots.

00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:49.510
While these two films demonstrated that adult-oriented
animation could be financially and critically

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:52.350
successful,
not much really changed in the wider perception

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:56.250
of animation.
These films were still essentially novelties.

00:12:56.250 --> 00:13:00.350
While there was some attempt by others to
capitalize on the success of Bakshi’s films

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:03.630
in America,
this mostly took the form of distributors

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:08.680
quickly repackaging English dubs of adult
animation from Japan and Europe.

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:12.750
Bakshi would follow this up with his most
controversial film, Coonskin,

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:18.190
an adaptation of the Uncle Remus stories transplanting
them from the rural American South into a

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:23.170
gangster story in Harlem.
While this movie occupies something of a place

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:26.090
alongside contemporary blaxploitation films
like

00:13:26.090 --> 00:13:29.470
Sweet Sweetback's Baadasssss Song, Dolemite,
and Shaft,

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:33.970
and has been praised for the depth of the
references in its adaptation,

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:38.520
such as including details from the African
myths that predate Uncle Remus,

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:43.460
it also heavily utilizes historical racist
caricature and minstrel imagery.

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:47.320
There’s a lot of debate about the artistic
intent behind the film,

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:52.680
whether or not the use of this imagery is
intended to shock and shame the white institutions

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:57.170
that created the imagery in the first place,
how effective it is at that,

00:13:57.170 --> 00:14:02.110
and whether or not protests of the film in
1975 were justified,

00:14:02.110 --> 00:14:07.620
but reflecting on it 45 years later the main
thing that stands out to me is a sort of fixation

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:12.200
at play,
like Bakshi just wanted to also make a blaxploitation

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:15.930
film,
a genre that sprang out of the kinds of neighbourhoods

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:19.839
that he grew up in,
and out of a community that he saw himself

00:14:19.839 --> 00:14:21.180
as a part of.

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:26.570
And reception was far from universal.
The Congress of Racial Equality protested

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:30.480
it,
the NAACP supported it as a “difficult satire”,

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:33.160
and according to Bakshi the Wu Tang Clan love
it.

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:38.150
It’s a very complicated intersection of
politics and influences that ultimately hinge

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:43.180
on the question of whether or not this was
Bakshi’s story to tell.

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:48.140
Modern commentators have compared it favorably
to Childish Gambino’s “This is America”

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:52.350
And I guess at the end of the day the meat
of the film is that the cops and the Mafia

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:53.720
suck.

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:57.830
[Indistinct Italian Noises]

00:14:57.830 --> 00:15:02.040
Coonskin was not successful, and did slow
the momentum on Bakshi’s career,

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:06.490
but it was still a relatively inexpensive
film and the failure was not a fatal blow

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:09.270
to his career as a director.

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:13.500
During post-production on Coonskin Bakshi
came up with the concept for Hey Good Lookin’,

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:16.371
another street film, this time set in the
fifties,

00:15:16.371 --> 00:15:20.430
but one in which animated and live-action
characters would interact.

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:23.770
Warner Brothers agreed to finance the film
in 1973,

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:29.020
and the live action footage was shot, largely
improvisationally, in early 74.

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:32.030
However the film would never be completed
as originally conceived,

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:37.339
as the process of having live action and animated
characters interact proved to be too labour

00:15:37.339 --> 00:15:41.290
intensive to complete on the film’s budget
of 1.5 million.

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:45.920
A rotoscoping rig was built at Bakshi’s
Brooklyn studio to try and speed up the animation

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:48.940
process,
but a series of conflicts between Bakshi and

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:53.000
Warner Brothers led to the film’s release
date being pushed back several times before

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:54.820
being shelved indefinitely.

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:59.400
While Hey Good Lookin’ was trapped in post-production
hell, Bakshi would release his first “family

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:03.320
friendly” feature, Wizards,
which is also a departure from his urban-life

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:06.250
focused films into an explicit genre film.

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:09.860
“Family friendly” is a bit of a weird
misnomer here, though, as the film is still

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:13.610
deliberately aimed at adults rather than an
all-ages crowd,

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:18.240
but it’s also a lot tamer with less sex,
gore, and profanity.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:21.930
The film was moderately successful, enough
to keep it from being considered a flop,

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:25.800
which is somewhat impressive given that the
movie is quite bad.

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:30.050
Aside from some notable iconography and some
compelling backgrounds the flow of the film

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:34.960
suffers from all the disjointed scene composition
of Bakshi’s earlier films.

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:39.130
While that works for Heavy Traffic, a film
about powerless characters trying to find

00:16:39.130 --> 00:16:43.870
their way in a disjointed world,
it really works against a film that’s so

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:50.370
plot-heavy there’s an entire movie's worth
of story dumped on the audience in the prologue.

00:16:50.370 --> 00:16:54.700
Illuminating history bearing on the everlasting
struggle for world supremacy

00:16:54.700 --> 00:17:01.230
The first blast was set off by five terrorists
It was a big day in Montegar

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:06.360
Delia felt a pull from the sky
The older fairy knew instantly that these

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:10.380
were not ordinary twins
The day will come, my brother, where I will

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:14.560
return and make this a planet where mutants
rule

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:19.350
Politically the film is bizarre.
It is clearly working through a lot of opinions

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:23.250
about the anti-war movement,
the protagonists are mostly twee fairies from

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:27.920
a literal fantasy land of mushrooms and rainbows
who are mowed down by machine gun wielding

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:32.690
mutants hopped up on Nazi propaganda
until the bearded wizard ends the war by shooting

00:17:32.690 --> 00:17:36.840
skeleton Hitler,
but those opinions haven’t been worked enough

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:43.590
to make them coherent or interesting,
and the end result flips rapidly between unbearably

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:45.950
treacle and deeply cynical.

00:17:45.950 --> 00:17:50.850
It’s not cohesive in style, the characters
look like they’re from completely different

00:17:50.850 --> 00:17:53.700
films,
it’s horny in a way that’s leering and

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:58.460
uncomfortable rather than sexy, and it’s
pretty boring.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:01.460
The most notable elements of the film are
all historical trivia.

00:18:01.460 --> 00:18:05.620
The film was being financed by Fox, and Bakshi
found himself in budget meetings with George

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:08.390
Lucas,
who was working on Star Wars at the time,

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:13.050
and the two became professional acquaintances.
George asked Ralph to change the working title

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:18.140
of War Wizards to avoid conflict with Star
Wars, and Ralph agreed because George let

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:21.929
Mark Hamil take time off from Star Wars to
record a part in Wizards.

00:18:21.929 --> 00:18:27.590
I’m Shaun, leader of the Knights of Stardust
and protectors of Dolan, king of the mountain

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:28.590
fairies.

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:32.610
It’s also the first film where Bakshi really
experimented with mixing in stylized live

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:36.720
action footage,
utilizing various rotoscoping and xerox techniques,

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:39.660
to save budget on animating large battle scenes.

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:43.630
The film was moderately successful, but Ralph’s
goodwill towards George Lucas came to an end

00:18:43.630 --> 00:18:47.140
when Star Wars,
released three weeks after Wizards, largely

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:49.040
replaced it in theatres.

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:52.820
Parallel to the production of most of these
films Bakshi pesters United Artists,

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:57.290
who have been stalling out on all their attempts
at getting a Lord of the Rings film rolling.

00:18:57.290 --> 00:19:03.140
Bakshi says he pitched UA on an animated Lord
of the Rings in 72 and 73, but they didn’t

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:06.530
bite.
Then in 75 he convinces Mike Medavoy to give

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:09.990
him a chance,
and Medavoy agrees, loosely, to two or three

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:16.100
films plus something Hobbit related.
Problem was United Artists already had a script

00:19:16.100 --> 00:19:22.920
written in 1970 by then-tv-writer John Boorman,
who at this point in 1975 had just written

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:24.410
and directed Zardoz.

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:26.970
The gun is good

00:19:26.970 --> 00:19:30.400
There was some conflict over the script, because,
[coughing]

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:36.410
well, it is absolutely buckwild with a Galadriel/Frodo
sex scene,

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:41.100
Aragorn and Boromir kissing passionately with
Arwen’s blood on their lips,

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:46.350
the history of the ring presented as a rock
opera at the Council of Elrons,

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:54.480
and Gimli is rebirthed in mud to recall the
ancient ancestral password to Moria.

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:58.980
Bakshi convinced Dan Melnyk at MGM to buy
out the project so that they could throw the

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:02.309
script out and start over, which they do.

00:20:02.309 --> 00:20:05.920
So Bakshi starts over on the script with novice
screenwriter Chris Conkling,

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:12.320
but when Dan Melnick gets ousted from MGM
in 1976 the new producer, Dick Shepherd, doesn’t

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:14.790
seem to know or care about the project at
all,

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:20.130
so Bakshi gets in touch with Saul Zaentz,
who had helped him finance Fritz back in 71,

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:25.429
and convinces him to buy out the project from
MGM, thus landing the thing back at United

00:20:25.429 --> 00:20:26.590
Artists.

00:20:26.590 --> 00:20:30.970
Incidentally Zaentz goes beyond this,
buying out the entirety of Tolkien’s film,

00:20:30.970 --> 00:20:34.730
stage, and merchandising rights,
which starts a chain reaction that would eventually

00:20:34.730 --> 00:20:38.310
lead, decades later,
to the troubled production that resulted in

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:39.960
The Hobbit: Battle of Five Armies

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:43.320
Why does it hurt so much?

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:47.390
Unsatisfied with Conkling’s work Bakshi
and Zaentz sideline him and hire Peter S.

00:20:47.390 --> 00:20:50.750
Beagle,
author of The Last Unicorn, to do a rewrite,

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:57.030
which Bakshi and Zaentz are mostly happy with,
and finally in 1976 a theatrical Lord of the

00:20:57.030 --> 00:20:59.470
Rings film is full-steam-ahead.

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:03.130
Before the movie even hits theatres, though,
it has two complications.

00:21:03.130 --> 00:21:06.140
The first is obviously Bakshi’s reputation
and style.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:10.130
Is Gandalf going to whip his dick out and
piss off the bridge of Khazad-Dum?

00:21:10.130 --> 00:21:13.500
Will pipe-weed be some dank bud?
Who knows.

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:18.820
The second is Rankin/Bass, an American production
company that mostly made seasonal television

00:21:18.820 --> 00:21:22.100
specials by outsourcing animation to Japan.

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:27.320
Rankin/Bass had been working on an adaptation
of The Hobbit as a TV special since 72, and

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:32.500
it was looking to broadcast by 77.
Additionally they had already storyboarded

00:21:32.500 --> 00:21:37.170
a sequel to their Hobbit film utilizing large
chunks of The Return of the King.

00:21:37.170 --> 00:21:41.920
Both of these would conflict with any Lord
of the Rings film United Artists produced,

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:46.170
particularly once Bakshi convinced them the
film could be done justice if it were animated,

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:48.670
since audiences would assume they were all
related.

00:21:48.670 --> 00:21:53.440
But, since the books were still public domain,
Rankin/Bass could do whatever they wanted,

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:57.799
at least within the US,
and a lawsuit to intervene succeeded only

00:21:57.799 --> 00:22:00.900
in securing a broadcast agreement in Canada.

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:05.980
Mm, my precious, will it taste delicious?

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:11.560
Bakshi’s production, even after settling
down at United Artists, was tumultuous,

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:15.700
but mostly in a way that’s probably better
described as indecisive.

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:19.670
The script was overhauled several times,
mostly because of arguments about how much

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:23.630
of the books should or could be adapted into
a single movie.

00:22:23.630 --> 00:22:26.170
Bakshi and Beagle ultimately pushed for two
movies,

00:22:26.170 --> 00:22:28.960
the first encompassing Fellowship and Two
Towers,

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:32.100
and intended for this film to be subtitled
Part 1,

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:35.480
but United Artists waffled on committing to
a second film.

00:22:35.480 --> 00:22:39.490
They didn’t outright say no, in a way that
would have pushed the production to commit

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:42.850
everything to one film,
or make it more conclusive,

00:22:42.850 --> 00:22:47.720
but they also left the fate of Part 2 in the
hazy realm of “let’s wait and see what

00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:48.750
happens.”

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:52.260
Of course as history has already borne out,
the sequel was never produced,

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:57.280
and the film ends with a disorientingly quick
resolution where Gandalf implies that the

00:22:57.280 --> 00:23:02.200
Battle of Helm’s Deep is in fact the deciding
moment of victory, end of story.

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:08.270
Released in 1978, reaction was lukewarm, but
broadly positive and not terribly harsh.

00:23:08.270 --> 00:23:12.750
Most of Bakshi’s idiosyncrasies as a director
are either absent or under control.

00:23:12.750 --> 00:23:17.100
Sort of.
It’s certainly the least juvenile, no one

00:23:17.100 --> 00:23:21.630
whips their dick out, everyone’s tits stay
inside their shirts, there’s no random comedy

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:27.750
skits inserted haphazardly to pad runtime,
and the adaptation is certainly faithful in

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:32.120
the sense that the vast majority of the dialogue
is copied directly from the books.

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:37.190
But, and this is probably its greatest flaw,
it still exhibits Bakshi’s inability to

00:23:37.190 --> 00:23:40.120
focus on the story at hand.

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:44.640
Jackson’s films, especially Fellowship,
are an illustrative comparison here.

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:49.460
Jackson’s films are focused and cohesive.
It’s an adventure story about big events

00:23:49.460 --> 00:23:54.660
and big emotions, the unbreakable bond of
friendship forged in adversity, the pain of

00:23:54.660 --> 00:23:56.910
loss, and swelling moments of triumph.

00:23:56.910 --> 00:24:06.380
You bow to no one.

00:24:06.380 --> 00:24:12.780
Jackson gets how the characters and plot interweave,
that it’s explicitly a story about how this

00:24:12.780 --> 00:24:17.530
big adventure changes the characters,
and if you don’t have both then you don’t

00:24:17.530 --> 00:24:19.000
have the whole.

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:25.000
So here’s the problem: Bakshi just isn’t
very good at plot or pacing.

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:30.010
He learned his craft working on slapstick
cartoons, and his first three films are effectively

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:34.690
just a series of vignettes.
Heavy Traffic is an urban slice-of-life film

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:39.760
and Fritz the Cat and Coonskin are both adapted
from explicitly episodic material.

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:43.240
This is not strictly a criticism, it works
in Heavy Traffic,

00:24:43.240 --> 00:24:48.440
it’s not a bad style, there’s nothing
wrong with vignette storytelling,

00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:50.890
it just needs the right material.

00:24:50.890 --> 00:24:56.380
But then you get Wizards, which is supposed
to be this really plot-driven adventure story,

00:24:56.380 --> 00:25:00.470
heavy on worldbuilding,
and it’s just a meandering mess.

00:25:00.470 --> 00:25:06.080
Unimportant skits drag for minutes,
action scenes repeat stock battle clips endlessly,

00:25:06.080 --> 00:25:09.040
and important moments resolve in seconds.

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:15.559
While less extreme than Wizards, this is unfortunately
the main failing of Lord of the Rings.

00:25:15.559 --> 00:25:22.440
Bakshi was, for most of the 70s, both extremely
busy, juggling multiple productions simultaneously,

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:27.721
and also allegedly on a lot of drugs.
It’s just not a state of mind that’s really

00:25:27.721 --> 00:25:34.250
conducive to making a film that maintains
a tight focus for two and a half hours, and

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:35.330
it shows.

00:25:35.330 --> 00:25:38.890
The film has a lot of content to try and fit
into its runtime,

00:25:38.890 --> 00:25:44.910
and yet the flight at the ford is an interminably
long prog rock jam session of Frodo falling

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:46.210
off a horse.

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:50.270
The story is presented very literally, lifted
straight from the novels,

00:25:50.270 --> 00:25:53.450
but with little weight for how it all connects
together.

00:25:53.450 --> 00:25:58.440
This creates a notable problem when the film
transitions from Fellowship into Two Towers,

00:25:58.440 --> 00:26:03.150
because the adaptation is so faithful to the
books that it feels like you’re at the end

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:06.880
of the movie, but it just keeps going.

00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:10.730
Also the second half of the film is pretty
weak.

00:26:10.730 --> 00:26:13.860
The sequences get really muddled, a lot of
threads are dropped,

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:16.570
presumably to have been picked up in part
2,

00:26:16.570 --> 00:26:21.750
and there are more and more animation shortcuts
taken as the production ran up against budget

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:22.810
constraints.

00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:27.760
On one hand, the sheer volume of roto done
on the battle of Helm’s Deep is already

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:30.640
immense,
but on the other hand there’s a lot of shots

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:36.580
like this where you can just outright see
that it’s a guy wearing rubber orc gloves.

00:26:36.580 --> 00:26:40.980
While otherwise the backgrounds in the film
range from gorgeously stylized paintings to

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:46.330
evocative abstract non-landscapes,
for most of the Battle of Helm’s Deep any

00:26:46.330 --> 00:26:52.690
background or distance is filled with stock
footage of clouds regardless of camera angle.

00:26:52.690 --> 00:26:58.200
On top of the shortcuts, Bakshi is just generally
not very good at keeping track of the action

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:02.400
and geography of his fight scenes,
making them really hard to follow,

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:06.230
and the muddy, high contrast artwork doesn’t
help.

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:11.580
Then at the end Gandalf rides in and the narrator
implies that this battle defeated Sauron,

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:15.210
but also maybe stay tuned for part two?

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:23.110
The forces of darkness were driven forever
by the valiant friends of Frodo. As their

00:27:23.110 --> 00:27:30.280
valiant battle ended, so too ends the first
great tale of The Lord of the Rings.

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:33.330
It’s not a strong ending.

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:38.750
The film mixes animation styles in a way that
reads like Bakshi was constantly experimenting

00:27:38.750 --> 00:27:45.030
on-the-fly and how a scene ends up looking
is dictated by what seemed like a cool idea

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:49.340
that week,
and while this mixed media style is interesting

00:27:49.340 --> 00:27:53.500
in its own way,
the inconsistency of it contributes to a sense

00:27:53.500 --> 00:27:58.110
that there wasn’t a committed idea everyone
was working towards,

00:27:58.110 --> 00:28:03.640
that the ultimate creative vision was driven
mostly by momentary fascinations.

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:08.130
For The Lord of the Rings Bakshi utilized
a hodgepodge of animation formats predominantly

00:28:08.130 --> 00:28:13.070
based on rotoscoping,
modifying live action footage to various degrees.

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:16.480
Some of this involves using the live action
footage as a trace-reference,

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:21.340
the final product being a complete replacement,
sometimes it’s a paint-over,

00:28:21.340 --> 00:28:26.210
effectively just augmenting the original footage
with details like eyes or fangs,

00:28:26.210 --> 00:28:31.169
and sometimes it’s effectively just a colourization
of a xerox of the original footage.

00:28:31.169 --> 00:28:37.130
And, no, that’s not being snide, an actual
process that was in use in the 60s, 70s, and

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:42.530
80s involved photocopying line work done on
paper onto cellophane allowing rougher pencil

00:28:42.530 --> 00:28:46.350
lines to be used without inking.
Earlier versions of this technique is what

00:28:46.350 --> 00:28:50.200
gives One Hundred and One Dalmatians its distinctly
ragged look.

00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:55.070
If used on a photograph, however, it crushes
most of the greyscale tones, flattening the

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:57.780
image to solid blacks and whites.

00:28:57.780 --> 00:29:02.440
The second major technique used is solarization,
which was recommended to him by the film’s

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:08.049
cinematographer Timothy Galfas.
Solarization, more accurately pseudo-solarization,

00:29:08.049 --> 00:29:11.970
is a tricky process where the black and white
film is partially developed,

00:29:11.970 --> 00:29:14.820
then instead of being sent through a process
called fixing,

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:18.580
the part of development that stabilizes the
film so it can be handled,

00:29:18.580 --> 00:29:23.380
the image is re-exposed to light, and sent
through the entire development process a second

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:24.380
time.

00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:28.750
This technique, applied to photochemical film,
is extremely difficult to control,

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:32.390
largely relying on trial and error to get
desirable results,

00:29:32.390 --> 00:29:36.340
but the successful end product is a partially
inverted image,

00:29:36.340 --> 00:29:41.039
with a common artefact being a strong border
across high contrast boundaries,

00:29:41.039 --> 00:29:44.020
which can look kinda like an inked outline.

00:29:44.020 --> 00:29:50.130
Bakshi felt this stylization process was sufficiently
animation-like that it would fit within the

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:53.630
movie and allow them to use footage of large
scale battles,

00:29:53.630 --> 00:29:59.169
which were ultimately faster and cheaper to
stage with actors in costumes than to hand-draw

00:29:59.169 --> 00:30:02.230
frame-by-frame, even from a reference.

00:30:02.230 --> 00:30:05.919
All of these different techniques are combined
to various degrees over the course of the

00:30:05.919 --> 00:30:09.240
movie.
Sometimes solarized footage is painted over,

00:30:09.240 --> 00:30:13.500
sometimes it’s merely colourized,
sometimes it’s just played as-is over a

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:14.500
coloured background.

00:30:14.500 --> 00:30:18.840
The extensive amount of rotoscoping and re-purposed
footage ultimately required the production

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:22.940
to shoot basically the entire film as live
action first,

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:26.990
with reference performers, stunt performers,
and the extensive battle scenes,

00:30:26.990 --> 00:30:33.070
so the two year production involved essentially
making the entire movie twice,

00:30:33.070 --> 00:30:37.100
first in the live action shoot in Madrid,
and second in the animation.

00:30:37.100 --> 00:30:40.150
There’s a somewhat apocryphal story in all
this.

00:30:40.150 --> 00:30:44.179
In shooting the footage they didn’t really
bother to clear backgrounds of things like

00:30:44.179 --> 00:30:49.800
telephone wires, cars, airplanes, bicycles,
and other obviously out-of-place elements,

00:30:49.800 --> 00:30:54.110
because it didn’t really matter, it wasn’t
the finished film anyway.

00:30:54.110 --> 00:30:58.610
According to Bakshi the Spanish developers
who were handling the camera negatives didn’t

00:30:58.610 --> 00:31:02.250
understand that the footage was a reference
that would be animated over top of,

00:31:02.250 --> 00:31:07.000
thought that this was, instead, incredibly
sloppy filmmaking, feared that it would give

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:10.850
Madrid a bad name,
and attempted to destroy the film.

00:31:10.850 --> 00:31:13.970
I’m repeating the story because it’s kinda
cute,

00:31:13.970 --> 00:31:20.330
but also it’s a bit too weird and sensational,
and the only source is Bakshi himself, who

00:31:20.330 --> 00:31:24.340
is, let’s just say, prone to exaggeration.

00:31:24.340 --> 00:31:28.040
Like he’ll say they had six hundred animators
working on Lord of the Rings when in reality

00:31:28.040 --> 00:31:29.520
it was more like fifty.

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:35.100
I was over in Spain shooting major live action
footage, got three thousand people in the

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:39.580
studio back in New York animating, I’m fielding
five hundred calls a day from the problems

00:31:39.580 --> 00:31:44.080
at the studio, I’m shooting an entire live
action movie, and I’m trying to eat dinner

00:31:44.080 --> 00:31:47.300
with Zaentz at night who wants to be talked
to.

00:31:47.300 --> 00:31:51.309
Or this bit from a 2006 interview with Underground
Online.

00:31:51.309 --> 00:31:55.191
“I had the X rating on my films and that
should have been enough to protect me. It

00:31:55.191 --> 00:31:59.110
was all a misunderstanding of me being too
far ahead of the curve. Now they do as much

00:31:59.110 --> 00:32:02.640
on The Simpsons as I got an X rating for Fritz
the Cat.”

00:32:02.640 --> 00:32:09.770
And, like… no? No Ralph. No they don’t.
What… What do you think happens on The Simpsons?

00:32:09.770 --> 00:32:14.100
I am very curious what Ralph Bakshi thinks
happens on The Simpsons.

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:18.520
On the whole the film is a mixed bag, there’s
a lot of jank, but what works? What does it

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:19.820
get right?

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:20.820
A lot, actually.

00:32:20.820 --> 00:32:26.270
Whoah, Sam Gamgee, your legs are too short
so use your head

00:32:26.270 --> 00:32:30.760
The vocal performances, in particular, are
generally good, often great.

00:32:30.760 --> 00:32:34.430
The voice actors do well with Tolkien’s
words, with an interpretation that is both

00:32:34.430 --> 00:32:36.360
distinct and appropriate.

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:39.960
One thing that’s often cited as a stand
out, though, is John Hurt’s performance

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:43.610
as Aragorn, and for good reason, it’s fantastic.

00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:46.550
“It matters. We still have a long road and
much to do.”

00:32:46.550 --> 00:32:49.800
“Why? We have no hope without Gandalf, you
know that Aragorn.”

00:32:49.800 --> 00:32:53.179
“Then we must do without hope! There is
always vengeance!”

00:32:53.179 --> 00:32:57.200
Gruff, yet warm, there’s a lot to love about
this performance.

00:32:57.200 --> 00:33:02.770
John Hurt was a great actor and he absolutely
has a world-weary charisma that really works

00:33:02.770 --> 00:33:04.870
here.
It’s fantastic. I love it.

00:33:04.870 --> 00:33:09.720
And it meshes well with Bakshi’s naturalistic
filmmaking sensibilities, this version of

00:33:09.720 --> 00:33:15.610
the characters that are not so much the protagonists
of a fantasy epic, but just some dudes trying

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:17.040
to solve a problem.

00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:20.309
“We have no choice, Aragorn!”
“We might go by way of the gap of Rohan”

00:33:20.309 --> 00:33:25.190
“That would take the ring too close to Isengard
and Aruman, we dare not risk it.”

00:33:25.190 --> 00:33:27.440
“And yet you would risk the mines of Moria”

00:33:27.440 --> 00:33:31.690
While Bakshi is bad at pacing and action,
he’s got a good sense for the interplay

00:33:31.690 --> 00:33:34.860
between characters,
and the film’s best moments come in snippets

00:33:34.860 --> 00:33:38.960
from these interactions,
the dynamics of conflict in dialogue, and

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:43.060
the small physical actions that punctuate
those moments.

00:33:43.060 --> 00:33:46.370
Scenes like Boromir’s death hold sufficient
dramatic weight,

00:33:46.370 --> 00:33:51.020
the reference acting, animation, and vocal
performances all come together and really

00:33:51.020 --> 00:33:54.750
work,
in a way that shows off the film at its strongest.

00:33:54.750 --> 00:33:59.140
Just the clink and clank of equipment, the
subtle atmospheric wind,

00:33:59.140 --> 00:34:04.900
and a mature tenderness as the three pay respect
to a fallen comrade.

00:34:04.900 --> 00:34:07.730
And there’s little moments, just great touches
of detail,

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:13.149
like Sam and Frodo paddling in opposite directions
as they debate the next course of action where

00:34:13.149 --> 00:34:17.569
the rhythm of it is spot on,
a fantastic little flare that communicates

00:34:17.569 --> 00:34:23.329
the emotion that underlies the dialogue.
It’s a keen physical detail that a lazier

00:34:23.329 --> 00:34:24.940
production would miss.

00:34:24.940 --> 00:34:30.069
The twitchy, feral movements of the black
riders is a weird creative decision, but I

00:34:30.069 --> 00:34:34.159
think it works.
It’s unsettling and menacing in an unusual

00:34:34.159 --> 00:34:36.989
way,
though it does get a little odd when the Nazgul

00:34:36.989 --> 00:34:41.229
simply stop behaving like this after the Prancing
Pony.

00:34:41.229 --> 00:34:43.639
Again, consistency is a problem.

00:34:43.639 --> 00:34:48.829
There’s also small adaptational decisions.
Lord of the Rings is so big and sprawling

00:34:48.829 --> 00:34:53.730
that basically any cinematic adaptation will
have to pick and choose what it includes and

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:57.410
what it doesn’t.
For as comprehensive as the Jackson films

00:34:57.410 --> 00:35:00.559
are there’s a lot they had to leave behind.

00:35:00.559 --> 00:35:03.809
Like this little moment, after Gandalf opens
the door to Moria.

00:35:03.809 --> 00:35:10.059
“so all you had to do was say ‘friend’
and enter”

00:35:10.059 --> 00:35:11.769
“Those were happier times”

00:35:11.769 --> 00:35:17.390
It’s a great little touch to include, because
the whole joke of the door to Moria, for the

00:35:17.390 --> 00:35:20.230
reader, is that they’re over-thinking the
problem,

00:35:20.230 --> 00:35:24.910
that the troubles facing the Fellowship, the
rise of Sauron in the East, has created a

00:35:24.910 --> 00:35:28.489
culture of fear, a culture of security and
paranoia,

00:35:28.489 --> 00:35:34.539
that leads Gandalf to assume the answer is
more complicated than it really is.

00:35:34.539 --> 00:35:39.390
It’s a melancholic point about how the people
of Middle Earth have grown apart, distrustful,

00:35:39.390 --> 00:35:43.579
and isolated,
to the point that even being asked to say

00:35:43.579 --> 00:35:48.200
“friend” feels like a trick.
It’s a good detail to include.

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:53.869
Bakshi’s film is basically the only adaptation
to include Frodo’s defiance of the Ring

00:35:53.869 --> 00:35:54.869
Wraiths at the ford.

00:35:54.869 --> 00:35:59.069
“By all the Shire, you shall have neither
the ring, nor me!”

00:35:59.069 --> 00:36:04.529
I also really like the introduction, presented
as a shadow play. It’s cheap and poorly

00:36:04.529 --> 00:36:09.260
acted and looks like community theatre, but
that’s what I find endearing about it,

00:36:09.260 --> 00:36:13.720
like it could just as well be an in-universe
performance of myth.

00:36:13.720 --> 00:36:17.920
You’ve got actors who are clearly trying
to avoid hurting each other with their prop

00:36:17.920 --> 00:36:21.210
swords,
and miming slow-motion instead of actually

00:36:21.210 --> 00:36:26.249
shooting the footage in slow motion,
and it’s clearly taking place on a stage,

00:36:26.249 --> 00:36:31.430
but the fact that it’s so evidently low-budget,
I dunno, I find it charming.

00:36:31.430 --> 00:36:35.099
Now, unfortunately, for all the things that
I do enjoy about this movie, all the things

00:36:35.099 --> 00:36:38.529
that I think work,
or are at least admirable for their ambition,

00:36:38.529 --> 00:36:43.670
there’s a lot that doesn’t,
either failing entirely or just not quite

00:36:43.670 --> 00:36:46.170
coming together into a cohesive whole.

00:36:46.170 --> 00:36:48.609
Like, for example, the Balrog.

00:36:48.609 --> 00:36:52.369
Alright, so, a bit of a side-track with the
Balrog here.

00:36:52.369 --> 00:36:56.979
One of the biggest running arguments in Tolkien
scholarship is this: does the Balrog actually

00:36:56.979 --> 00:37:02.249
have wings or does it merely have a form that
is evocative of wings?

00:37:02.249 --> 00:37:06.009
Or does it have neither wings nor a form evocative
of wings,

00:37:06.009 --> 00:37:11.710
but an incorporeal aura of darkness that projects
the impression of wings without being a component

00:37:11.710 --> 00:37:15.219
part of the substance or form of the Balrog’s
essential self?

00:37:15.219 --> 00:37:19.369
The relevant passages from the book are in
The Fellowship of the Ring, where first Tolkien

00:37:19.369 --> 00:37:22.739
writes about the Balrog
“... the shadow about it reached out like

00:37:22.739 --> 00:37:25.569
two vast wings...”
and a couple paragraphs later

00:37:25.569 --> 00:37:30.630
“It stepped forward onto the bridge, and
suddenly it drew itself up to a great height,

00:37:30.630 --> 00:37:33.290
and its wings were spread from wall to wall;”

00:37:33.290 --> 00:37:37.680
This apparent conflict between a stylistic
description and a literal description has

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:42.410
formed the foundation of a half-century long
debate over the intended physical properties

00:37:42.410 --> 00:37:44.180
of a mythological demon.

00:37:44.180 --> 00:37:49.330
As of June 2021 the Tolkien Society FAQ still
has, as the top entry, “Do Balrogs have

00:37:49.330 --> 00:37:53.229
Wings? Can they Fly?”
which they summarize with “that’s up to

00:37:53.229 --> 00:37:55.170
each individual reader to decide.”

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:59.609
Quora, the spiritual successor to Yahoo Answers,
has multiple threads on the subject.

00:37:59.609 --> 00:38:04.390
Bakshi, perhaps unknowingly, stepped right
in this when he gave the Balrog big old bat

00:38:04.390 --> 00:38:07.160
wings,
with a Balrog that’s definitely reminiscent

00:38:07.160 --> 00:38:12.570
of the Hildebrandt brothers’ Balrog from
the 1977 Tolkien art calendar.

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:16.779
The 1987 calendar featured a wingless Balrog
painted by Tolkien scholar Ted Nasmith.

00:38:16.779 --> 00:38:21.819
John Howe’s 1996 painting “Gandalf Falls
With the Balrog” features a distinctly bat-winged

00:38:21.819 --> 00:38:22.819
demon.

00:38:22.819 --> 00:38:27.819
Peter Jackson threaded the needle with a Balrog
that is as much a smoke monster as it is physical,

00:38:27.819 --> 00:38:29.989
though it still definitely has wings.

00:38:29.989 --> 00:38:34.160
Video games also alternate between wings and
no wings.

00:38:34.160 --> 00:38:38.410
Tolkien hack David Day’s “A Tolkien Bestiary”
indicates no wings, while Robert Foster’s

00:38:38.410 --> 00:38:40.770
authoritative “The Complete Guide to Middle
Earth” is mum on the subject.

00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:46.109
The online Encyclopedia of Arda, dating back
to 1997, spends four fifths of its word count

00:38:46.109 --> 00:38:50.220
for the entry on Balrogs summarizing both
the pro- and anti- wing arguments,

00:38:50.220 --> 00:38:53.910
though ultimately errs on the side of no wings
without taking a definitive stance.

00:38:53.910 --> 00:38:57.250
The start of this argument, naturally, just
spurs further arguments.

00:38:57.250 --> 00:39:01.529
The Balrog were created with intent by Melkor,
therefore vestigial wings would be illogical,

00:39:01.529 --> 00:39:06.009
and if the Balrog has wings then surely it
wouldn’t just plummet when the bridge collapses,

00:39:06.009 --> 00:39:08.220
which leads to arguments about the nature
of wings themselves,

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:12.430
since, after all, even if it has wings it’s
not a helicopter or a hummingbird, and probably

00:39:12.430 --> 00:39:15.359
couldn’t just hover.
Penguins, chickens, and emu all have wings,

00:39:15.359 --> 00:39:18.839
but they would plummet.
Even flighted birds like condor, and albatross

00:39:18.839 --> 00:39:20.950
can’t just take off from a standstill.

00:39:20.950 --> 00:39:24.830
But this argument also neglects to consider
that both Melkor and the Balrog were created

00:39:24.830 --> 00:39:27.930
with intent by one
honourable mister Sir Jolkien Rolkien Rolkien

00:39:27.930 --> 00:39:31.869
Tolkien and wings are both rad and badass,
functional or not.

00:39:31.869 --> 00:39:36.890
And anyway if Balrog have wings why couldn’t
they just fly the ring into Mordor?

00:39:36.890 --> 00:39:40.069
Now, the wings are not something that particularly
bothers me.

00:39:40.069 --> 00:39:44.549
The Hildebrandt painting is actually my earliest
memory of Tolkien, period, as it’s the cover

00:39:44.549 --> 00:39:48.289
of the book Art of the Brothers Hildebrandt,
and we had a copy kicking around the house

00:39:48.289 --> 00:39:53.510
when I was a kid, so this is already a formative
vision of the scene for me.

00:39:53.510 --> 00:39:58.720
Clearly the actual fact of wings is secondary
to the narrative functionality of the evocative

00:39:58.720 --> 00:40:03.660
image of Gandalf as a point of light standing
off against an enveloping darkness.

00:40:03.660 --> 00:40:09.079
The actual problems with the Balrog here in
Bakshi’s version aren’t wings, in and

00:40:09.079 --> 00:40:13.450
of themselves,
but that the design just doesn’t come together

00:40:13.450 --> 00:40:21.989
and, most importantly, is really poorly animated.
Bakshi, as an animator, is not particularly

00:40:21.989 --> 00:40:24.049
good at momentum.

00:40:24.049 --> 00:40:28.160
Motion and momentum are core elements of animation,
and it’s something that Bakshi has always

00:40:28.160 --> 00:40:31.029
struggled with.
Part of his experimentation with rotoscoping

00:40:31.029 --> 00:40:34.509
was tied to this.
It’s a cost-saving measure, but also it

00:40:34.509 --> 00:40:40.729
means the momentum problem solves itself.
You have real footage to work from, the momentum

00:40:40.729 --> 00:40:44.359
is already real, it’s done for you, boom.

00:40:44.359 --> 00:40:48.509
Of course animated momentum and real momentum
aren’t the same thing,

00:40:48.509 --> 00:40:52.250
and your rotoscope is only going to look as
good as your source footage,

00:40:52.250 --> 00:40:56.979
which is going to be really hard to get right
if you don’t have a twelve foot tall Balrog

00:40:56.979 --> 00:40:58.910
to shoot some reference footage of.

00:40:58.910 --> 00:41:03.999
This is a moment where the realism of rotoscoping
is absolutely undermining the final product

00:41:03.999 --> 00:41:09.999
because we are, unfortunately, seeing through
to the underlying reality of man in a costume

00:41:09.999 --> 00:41:14.849
trying to mime being really big by just moving
slowly.

00:41:14.849 --> 00:41:20.180
It’s not dynamic, it’s not threatening,
and it comes off as unfortunately goofy.

00:41:20.180 --> 00:41:24.119
This is a running thing through the film,
most of the action scenes lack a sense of

00:41:24.119 --> 00:41:27.989
weight to their movement.
The actors are just lightly swinging their

00:41:27.989 --> 00:41:33.489
prop swords at each other, pulling their punches
because, you know, it’s just a reference.

00:41:33.489 --> 00:41:39.380
But since the reference is being traced frame-by-frame
that performance carries through to the final

00:41:39.380 --> 00:41:42.079
animation.
Sometimes the rotoscope inherits a really

00:41:42.079 --> 00:41:46.779
effective sense of weight and sometimes it
ends up looking cheap and fake.

00:41:46.779 --> 00:41:51.140
This also leaves behind a number of strange
artefacts in how shots are framed.

00:41:51.140 --> 00:41:55.900
They’re few and far between, but there’s
the occasional shot where the framing is oddly

00:41:55.900 --> 00:42:01.579
tight, where characters drift out of frame
in a way that’s highly unusual for animation,

00:42:01.579 --> 00:42:05.809
where the positioning of characters is normally
extremely deliberate.

00:42:05.809 --> 00:42:11.589
The pitfall of a rotoscoped film is that the
final results depend heavily on your references.

00:42:11.589 --> 00:42:15.940
The reference performers aren’t simply providing
something to help the animators get the right

00:42:15.940 --> 00:42:23.249
idea, their performance is the performance,
and there’s a definite inconsistency here.

00:42:23.249 --> 00:42:26.390
And that’s really the word of the day, isn’t
it?

00:42:26.390 --> 00:42:31.099
The biggest failings of Bakshi’s Lord of
the Rings are matters of inconsistency.

00:42:31.099 --> 00:42:34.279
Sometimes the reference actors are giving
it their all, and sometimes they’re just

00:42:34.279 --> 00:42:38.150
loosely miming the actions.
Sometimes footage is shot in slow motion and

00:42:38.150 --> 00:42:41.390
other times the actors just swing their swords
slowly.

00:42:41.390 --> 00:42:45.920
Scenes change style and grain and texture
on the fly, and characters are animated in

00:42:45.920 --> 00:42:49.220
multiple different styles across the film
as a whole.

00:42:49.220 --> 00:42:54.150
Heck, sometimes characters switch techniques
multiple times within the span of a few seconds,

00:42:54.150 --> 00:42:56.569
as with Aragorn running down this hallway.

00:42:56.569 --> 00:43:00.609
There’s also a thing that happens a few
times where the scenes were too complicated

00:43:00.609 --> 00:43:04.279
to fully animate,
like Merry and Pippin fighting the orcs before

00:43:04.279 --> 00:43:08.819
their capture,
but the underlying footage is in really bad

00:43:08.819 --> 00:43:12.630
shape:
super high contrast, super under-exposed,

00:43:12.630 --> 00:43:17.160
super grainy, and the whole image just turns
into an indecipherable blob.

00:43:17.160 --> 00:43:21.670
A much-commented on quirk of the movie is
that a lot of characters wave their hands

00:43:21.670 --> 00:43:26.020
around really aimlessly.
This is going to come down to a direction

00:43:26.020 --> 00:43:31.140
issue, as it’s a very community theatre
kind of quirk of acting, with untrained actors

00:43:31.140 --> 00:43:33.329
over-using their hands.

00:43:33.329 --> 00:43:36.380
The Nazgul and orcs being mostly paint-over
work,

00:43:36.380 --> 00:43:40.089
with much of the actual costumes still visible,
works,

00:43:40.089 --> 00:43:45.450
but is undercut by the fact that the fellowship,
too, are often animated as paint-overs, with

00:43:45.450 --> 00:43:49.140
their live action counterparts being extremely
visible.

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:53.650
Even the full replacement trace-overs aren’t
without their own oddities, as the style is

00:43:53.650 --> 00:43:58.839
so chaotic, with linework that squiggles a
lot between frames, that it’s extremely

00:43:58.839 --> 00:44:05.119
intrusive when characters stop moving entirely,
becoming unnaturally still for a few frames,

00:44:05.119 --> 00:44:08.620
between actions.
That’s a limitation of budget, yes, and

00:44:08.620 --> 00:44:11.940
I don’t begrudge the animators for saving
those frames,

00:44:11.940 --> 00:44:17.380
but the style very much accentuates the effect
and calls attention to it.

00:44:17.380 --> 00:44:21.559
Treebeard is pretty much the only character
in the film that’s entirely animated from

00:44:21.559 --> 00:44:24.460
scratch,
which places him out-of-place at the other

00:44:24.460 --> 00:44:28.930
extreme end of the spectrum,
being very fluid and morphy, traditionally

00:44:28.930 --> 00:44:32.109
cartoonish,
looking more like an outcast from an Atkinson

00:44:32.109 --> 00:44:37.339
production like the Racoons than the comparably
heavy animation of the rest of the film.

00:44:37.339 --> 00:44:41.220
And while that heavier animation generally
looks really neat,

00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:45.079
the increased fidelity will, again, work against
the film,

00:44:45.079 --> 00:44:49.969
as any time the lip sync is off it feels really
off.

00:44:49.969 --> 00:44:51.969
“Whereout I to start?”

00:44:51.969 --> 00:44:57.420
There’s also an issue with the dialogue
that, well, descriptively the dialogue in

00:44:57.420 --> 00:45:02.359
a lot of places is stilted, and the recording
is thin.

00:45:02.359 --> 00:45:07.400
The micro-pacing of dialogue that makes it
feel natural, that makes it flow, it’s not

00:45:07.400 --> 00:45:11.140
always there,
and a lot of unspoken vocalizations are missing,

00:45:11.140 --> 00:45:14.430
which can make conversations drag and feel
unnatural.

00:45:14.430 --> 00:45:21.970
“I’ll give it to you Gandalf! You’re
wise and powerful. Will you not”

00:45:21.970 --> 00:45:24.880
“No! Do not tempt me!”

00:45:24.880 --> 00:45:30.180
This is a result of the production process.
There’s two factors here: for I guess budgetary

00:45:30.180 --> 00:45:35.329
reasons they apparently didn’t have a multi-track
recorder, and also the voice actors are ultimately

00:45:35.329 --> 00:45:40.849
having their performance superimposed on the
performance of the live action actors,

00:45:40.849 --> 00:45:45.900
which is substantially different from other
methods of animation where either the animators

00:45:45.900 --> 00:45:49.920
work from the actor’s performance
or the actor matches the performance created

00:45:49.920 --> 00:45:53.180
by the animators.
And so while the production opted to do the

00:45:53.180 --> 00:45:58.219
recording sessions with the cast as a group,
according to Anthony Daniels the actors were

00:45:58.219 --> 00:46:04.190
required to leave a long two-second pause
between each other’s lines so that the editors

00:46:04.190 --> 00:46:06.839
could try and line the two performances up.

00:46:06.839 --> 00:46:11.289
I mean, I can see the logic there.
Like you assume you’ve got this process

00:46:11.289 --> 00:46:15.190
that affords you a lot of freedom,
you don’t need to wait for one part to be

00:46:15.190 --> 00:46:20.450
done so that the other half can match it,
you can just do both halves whenever it’s

00:46:20.450 --> 00:46:26.779
convenient and then merge them later,
but, you know, it’s the details that get

00:46:26.779 --> 00:46:28.690
lost in that process.

00:46:28.690 --> 00:46:32.349
On the whole the film’s pacing is just really
off.

00:46:32.349 --> 00:46:37.349
Some sequences, like the flight to the ford
mentioned before go on at seemingly an interminable

00:46:37.349 --> 00:46:40.019
length,
while the entire second half of the film is

00:46:40.019 --> 00:46:44.099
incredibly rushed.
Even odd one-off moments will end up bizarrely

00:46:44.099 --> 00:46:47.180
truncated,
like the smoke trailing out of Moria behind

00:46:47.180 --> 00:46:50.750
the Fellowship,
which flashes on screen so briefly I wasn’t

00:46:50.750 --> 00:46:53.369
sure if I had bumped the remote and skipped
a scene.

00:46:53.369 --> 00:46:58.160
Merry and Pippin vanish from the film entirely
after meeting Treebeard, a casualty of the

00:46:58.160 --> 00:47:01.570
unproduced sequel,
but regardless of the intent their exit is

00:47:01.570 --> 00:47:05.729
undeniably sudden.
Likewise Sam and Frodo meet Gollum, set off

00:47:05.729 --> 00:47:09.630
towards the Dead Marshes, and are never seen
or mentioned again.

00:47:09.630 --> 00:47:14.079
One particular oddity is that Saruman is alternately
called either Saruman or Aruman.

00:47:14.079 --> 00:47:16.869
“I must go south now, to consult with the
wizard Aruman”

00:47:16.869 --> 00:47:22.059
“I have come for your aid, Saruman the White,
in troubled times”

00:47:22.059 --> 00:47:27.240
This bizarre inconsistency is the result of
Saul Zaentz’ insistence that the names of

00:47:27.240 --> 00:47:31.039
the antagonists, Saron and Saruman, sounded
too alike,

00:47:31.039 --> 00:47:39.509
which is fair enough as an adaptation change,
but then during fairly routine rewrites mid-production

00:47:39.509 --> 00:47:43.390
Beagle began swapping the names back to Saruman.

00:47:43.390 --> 00:47:46.940
“Saruman of Many Colours!”

00:47:46.940 --> 00:47:50.959
If there is something you can say is missing
from Bakshi’s Middle Earth it would be Middle

00:47:50.959 --> 00:47:54.200
Earth itself.
This is, perhaps, where the comparison between

00:47:54.200 --> 00:47:59.099
Bakshi and Jackson is the starkest.
While much of this is an argument of adaptational

00:47:59.099 --> 00:48:04.760
preference, which lines and details were included,
which phrases and character traits were stressed,

00:48:04.760 --> 00:48:10.670
one area where the older film is undeniably
weaker is in the presence of the world.

00:48:10.670 --> 00:48:15.880
And this is a meaningful absence.
Place is critical to the story of Lord of

00:48:15.880 --> 00:48:20.680
the Rings because Lord of the Rings is as
much a story about violence against the land

00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:25.329
itself as it is about violence against the
people who live on it.

00:48:25.329 --> 00:48:29.640
And while Bakshi’s artists are able to visualize
many iconic locations,

00:48:29.640 --> 00:48:35.299
both the fantastic and the quaint, just as
often the background dissolves away into an

00:48:35.299 --> 00:48:38.469
abstraction, into nowhere in particular.

00:48:38.469 --> 00:48:43.239
Though there is an isolated artistry to these
compositions, as a storytelling mechanism,

00:48:43.239 --> 00:48:47.539
as an expression of the text,
they just don’t compete with Jackson’s

00:48:47.539 --> 00:48:53.719
camera turned towards the beauty of New Zealand.
This gaze, importantly, retains the essence

00:48:53.719 --> 00:48:58.200
of the message:
the world is good, the world is beautiful,

00:48:58.200 --> 00:49:03.839
the world is worth saving, and not just the
so-called “important” parts.

00:49:03.839 --> 00:49:09.239
Tolkien’s notoriously florid descriptions
are just as reverent of grassland and marsh

00:49:09.239 --> 00:49:12.160
as they are of forest and mountain.

00:49:12.160 --> 00:49:15.180
This is the biggest missed step of the old
adaptation,

00:49:15.180 --> 00:49:20.069
the vision of Middle Earth not just as a land
under assault from a malevolent spirit seeking

00:49:20.069 --> 00:49:24.489
power,
but a land besieged by the smog and consumption

00:49:24.489 --> 00:49:27.410
and poisonous runoff of industry.

00:49:27.410 --> 00:49:31.200
Ultimately the biggest flaw of the film is
that it’s kinda boring.

00:49:31.200 --> 00:49:36.279
Not uninteresting, but all these issues add
up to long stretches of the film that just

00:49:36.279 --> 00:49:41.589
aren’t particularly noteworthy.
There is, at least in my opinion, very little

00:49:41.589 --> 00:49:44.469
after the death of Boromir that’s really
worth it,

00:49:44.469 --> 00:49:49.170
and given that his funeral is eighty-five
minutes into the movie, not only is it a clear

00:49:49.170 --> 00:49:52.230
demarcation point between two parts of the
story,

00:49:52.230 --> 00:49:58.980
it’s already a decent feature length, so
if you kinda check out there I don’t really

00:49:58.980 --> 00:49:59.980
blame you.

00:49:59.980 --> 00:50:02.950
There’s also a deeper issue that kinda cuts
two ways,

00:50:02.950 --> 00:50:06.730
and it’s that the film relies a lot on an
understanding of the source material.

00:50:06.730 --> 00:50:11.200
Now, I don’t think this is a conscious reliance,
I do think that Bakshi and Conkling and Beagle

00:50:11.200 --> 00:50:15.390
tried to create a telling of the story that’s
self-contained,

00:50:15.390 --> 00:50:19.079
but there’s enough holes, enough things
that are breezed past,

00:50:19.079 --> 00:50:22.339
that there’s definitely the sense that things
are missing,

00:50:22.339 --> 00:50:26.289
the keen awareness that this is an abridgement
of a much larger book,

00:50:26.289 --> 00:50:31.000
and so bits are included for the sake of being
comprehensive rather than because they make

00:50:31.000 --> 00:50:34.309
the best version of the story for the medium.

00:50:34.309 --> 00:50:38.660
I said this cuts two ways and that’s because
while this can make for an unsatisfying viewing

00:50:38.660 --> 00:50:42.170
on its own,
it can also, potentially, make for a satisfying

00:50:42.170 --> 00:50:45.359
companion to the novel,
where the viewer’s own knowledge of the

00:50:45.359 --> 00:50:50.779
text is able to fill the gaps and their imagination
is able to do the heavy lifting of fleshing

00:50:50.779 --> 00:50:54.999
it all out,
using the movie as an aide in their own internal

00:50:54.999 --> 00:50:58.199
visualization and realization of the story.

00:50:58.199 --> 00:51:01.920
Part of the trouble in researching the film
is that based on Bakshi’s own recollections

00:51:01.920 --> 00:51:06.660
of the film it’s not even entirely clear
when they decided to animate the film,

00:51:06.660 --> 00:51:11.719
or if the whole film was meant to be conventionally
animated with only a bit of rotoscoping but

00:51:11.719 --> 00:51:15.109
then they decided to rotoscope nearly the
entire thing,

00:51:15.109 --> 00:51:19.750
or if at one point they were even considering
cutting the animation entirely and just making

00:51:19.750 --> 00:51:23.079
a live-action film.
These were, apparently, decisions that were

00:51:23.079 --> 00:51:30.059
made more or less on the fly in 1976, a reflection
of the problems plaguing the still-unfinished

00:51:30.059 --> 00:51:31.059
Hey Good Lookin’.

00:51:31.059 --> 00:51:35.719
I don’t want to say that this is a film
made by filmmakers who didn’t care,

00:51:35.719 --> 00:51:40.930
who didn’t get the source material.
It is a film that’s lovingly made, it is

00:51:40.930 --> 00:51:46.470
a film made by creatives who cared,
the script is clearly intimately familiar

00:51:46.470 --> 00:51:50.349
with the source material,
but it also seems like a film that was made

00:51:50.349 --> 00:51:55.750
by creatives who were very distracted,
who didn’t have a strong vision, and were

00:51:55.750 --> 00:52:01.730
focused principally on working quickly and
making whatever compromises were needed just

00:52:01.730 --> 00:52:02.920
to get things done.

00:52:02.920 --> 00:52:08.279
And, to be clear, that’s not a moral failing,
it’s not a sin to be more concerned with

00:52:08.279 --> 00:52:11.400
getting the film done,
getting it in front of audiences,

00:52:11.400 --> 00:52:17.089
than picking fights with the studio.
They turned around a two and a half hour animated

00:52:17.089 --> 00:52:20.280
film in two years.
That’s insane.

00:52:20.280 --> 00:52:24.039
It’s amazing that the whole thing didn’t
entirely self-destruct,

00:52:24.039 --> 00:52:31.410
that the final result is not only reasonably
watchable, but often interesting and occasionally

00:52:31.410 --> 00:52:33.799
brilliant.
That’s impressive.

00:52:33.799 --> 00:52:38.900
So, the movie comes out with the title The
Lord of the Rings, no “part 1” subtitle.

00:52:38.900 --> 00:52:42.509
United Artists felt that no one would want
to pay to see half a story.

00:52:42.509 --> 00:52:46.750
Of course that seems ridiculous today, what
with film being so thoroughly dominated by

00:52:46.750 --> 00:52:52.119
serial franchises,
but in 1978 the concern was still sensible.

00:52:52.119 --> 00:52:56.469
The two part film didn’t really exist yet,
and even franchises were sparse,

00:52:56.469 --> 00:53:00.869
and more along the lines of James Bond,
a loosely connected episodic rather than a

00:53:00.869 --> 00:53:04.079
single cohesive story with meaningful continuity.

00:53:04.079 --> 00:53:09.190
But, still, a “to be continued” would
not have seriously shocked audiences.

00:53:09.190 --> 00:53:13.259
While Bakshi had done some press where he
was able to talk about how they’ll hopefully

00:53:13.259 --> 00:53:15.799
get to make the rest of the story with part
2,

00:53:15.799 --> 00:53:19.069
the media landscape is entirely different
in 1978.

00:53:19.069 --> 00:53:24.019
There isn’t a massive ecosystem of entertainment
news, there’s no widespread internet,

00:53:24.019 --> 00:53:27.729
there’s no fan blogs hanging off every detail
of production,

00:53:27.729 --> 00:53:32.410
so the general audience impression going in
is that this is the whole thing.

00:53:32.410 --> 00:53:36.559
Fans of the book are, of course, caught off
guard by the ending, the story just stopping

00:53:36.559 --> 00:53:40.479
after the Battle of Helm’s Deep,
and they’re not super happy about that,

00:53:40.479 --> 00:53:45.219
but on the whole audiences are pretty receptive
and the film does well.

00:53:45.219 --> 00:53:48.640
Critics are lukewarm but consensus is ultimately
positive.

00:53:48.640 --> 00:53:52.160
Roger Ebert’s bottom line summary is, I
think, right on the money.

00:53:52.160 --> 00:53:57.079
“In sum, Bakshi has succeeded better at
bringing Tolkien's characters to life than

00:53:57.079 --> 00:53:59.130
at bringing his story to fruition.”

00:53:59.130 --> 00:54:03.779
And that’s kinda where things have stayed.
Critical reevaluation hasn’t really changed

00:54:03.779 --> 00:54:08.290
over the decades since.
It’s flawed, mostly boring, but not entirely

00:54:08.290 --> 00:54:12.140
devoid of charm.
It’s quieter and stiffer than Jackson’s

00:54:12.140 --> 00:54:17.170
high-intensity action/adventure,
but that’s not wholly inappropriate as Tolkien’s

00:54:17.170 --> 00:54:21.410
books are, themselves, so very often quiet
and stiff.

00:54:21.410 --> 00:54:26.539
The film was successful, it turned a reasonable
profit, but it wasn’t a runaway success.

00:54:26.539 --> 00:54:29.959
Bakshi was feeling burnt out on working on
someone else’s story,

00:54:29.959 --> 00:54:34.890
and leadership changes at United Artists in
1978 proved to be enough of an interruption

00:54:34.890 --> 00:54:39.940
to the momentum of the project that attempts
to get Part 2 moving just fizzled out.

00:54:39.940 --> 00:54:44.289
Bakshi would continue to use rotoscoped animation
for three of his next four films,

00:54:44.289 --> 00:54:48.729
though audience interest waned as the style
grew increasingly dated compared to the

00:54:48.729 --> 00:54:52.559
lush and intricate animation of its big budget
contemporaries

00:54:52.559 --> 00:54:56.449
and as Bakshi seemingly ran out of energy
and ideas.

00:54:56.449 --> 00:55:02.109
Hey Good Lookin’ was eventually released
in 1982 in a totally overhauled format,

00:55:02.109 --> 00:55:06.690
the film having been essentially re-made as
a totally animated feature over the course

00:55:06.690 --> 00:55:10.509
of seven years,
financed by Bakshi himself, though little

00:55:10.509 --> 00:55:15.019
of the rotoscoping remained.
It is, for the most part, just a worse version

00:55:15.019 --> 00:55:18.789
of Heavy Traffic,
lacking the incendiary politics and righteous

00:55:18.789 --> 00:55:20.989
anger that gives that film its bite.

00:55:20.989 --> 00:55:24.259
I dunno, maybe it was just Bakshi getting
older,

00:55:24.259 --> 00:55:28.680
maybe you just couldn’t sell an anti-cop
movie in Reagan’s America.

00:55:28.680 --> 00:55:33.369
He eventually retired from feature films after
the flop of Cool World in 1992,

00:55:33.369 --> 00:55:39.109
a film that was, ironically, not nearly as
crass as audiences had hoped.

00:55:39.109 --> 00:55:44.009
But as a pure quirk of coincidence the animated
legacy of The Lord of the Rings isn’t entirely

00:55:44.009 --> 00:55:46.640
incomplete,
because Rankin/Bass, leveraging the public

00:55:46.640 --> 00:55:50.239
domain status of the books,
aired the sequel to their Hobbit adaptation

00:55:50.239 --> 00:55:53.630
in 1980,
and it just so happens to more or less pick

00:55:53.630 --> 00:55:58.299
up shortly after Bakshi’s film ends.
The specifics here are disputed, since the

00:55:58.299 --> 00:56:02.599
Rankin/Bass Return of the King had been storyboarded
years earlier,

00:56:02.599 --> 00:56:07.170
but it also didn’t really start serious
production until 78.

00:56:07.170 --> 00:56:12.789
So while the film wasn’t intended to capitalize
on the cancellation of Bakshi’s second film,

00:56:12.789 --> 00:56:14.569
it still did.

00:56:14.569 --> 00:56:17.469
Mostly bad in an annoying way and very cheaply
made,

00:56:17.469 --> 00:56:21.449
this TV movie is largely unmemorable save
for the absolute banger “Where There’s

00:56:21.449 --> 00:56:22.449
a Whip There’s a Way”

00:56:22.449 --> 00:56:27.140
“where there’s a whip [whipcrack] there’s
a way”

00:56:27.140 --> 00:56:31.099
Of course then, a little over twenty years
later, Peter Jackson, that guy who makes perverted

00:56:31.099 --> 00:56:34.150
puppet movies,
would finally get to make a no-holds-barred

00:56:34.150 --> 00:56:39.660
adaptation of The Lord of the Rings and it’s
really good.

00:56:39.660 --> 00:56:45.920
“Come on Mister Frodo, I can’t carry it
for you, but I can carry you!”

00:56:45.920 --> 00:56:49.349
Ultimately the legacy of Bakshi’s film is
in technology.

00:56:49.349 --> 00:56:51.930
Techniques that were odd and unique are, today,
routine.

00:56:51.930 --> 00:56:57.519
He didn’t invent any of them, strictly speaking,
the underpinning technology was already decades

00:56:57.519 --> 00:57:00.959
old,
but the haphazard, experimental, ambitious

00:57:00.959 --> 00:57:04.759
way that they’re applied,
the mix of success and failure, is ahead of

00:57:04.759 --> 00:57:08.069
its time,
presaging the ways that filmmaking was changing

00:57:08.069 --> 00:57:12.059
and would continue to change.
This isn’t to say that Bakshi and his animators

00:57:12.059 --> 00:57:16.920
changed the arc of history,
but rather they saw what was inevitable about

00:57:16.920 --> 00:57:21.959
the way that these technologies would be applied,
and bit off far more than they could chew

00:57:21.959 --> 00:57:24.469
decades before the tech was actually ready.

00:57:24.469 --> 00:57:28.999
Bakshi understood that the greatest limitation
of realizing the world of Tolkien was the

00:57:28.999 --> 00:57:32.430
world itself,
and he solved this problem by cutting out

00:57:32.430 --> 00:57:35.640
photographs of actors, maybe painting on them
a little,

00:57:35.640 --> 00:57:41.459
and placing them into animated environments,
and that right there describes basically every

00:57:41.459 --> 00:57:42.729
Marvel movie.

00:57:42.729 --> 00:57:47.410
For twenty years now it’s been routine for
actors to work against worlds that they can’t

00:57:47.410 --> 00:57:51.450
see, that are created out of whole cloth by
animators.

00:57:51.450 --> 00:57:57.329
The modern look of films is defined by actors
on set wearing some combination of costume

00:57:57.329 --> 00:58:00.779
that’s limiting or suggestive of the final
look

00:58:00.779 --> 00:58:06.279
before artists go in and paint the rest of
the costume on.

00:58:06.279 --> 00:58:10.980
Taking a physical performance and duplicating
it with an animated simulacrum,

00:58:10.980 --> 00:58:16.799
once the odd fixation of a few weirdos from
Brooklyn, is now ordinary.

00:58:16.799 --> 00:58:21.579
In a weird way Bakshi’s Lord of the Rings
is a twenty-first century blockbuster made

00:58:21.579 --> 00:58:23.809
with 1970s technology.

00:58:23.809 --> 00:58:29.270
And, in the end, he would ultimately be vindicated.
The argument that animation could be more

00:58:29.270 --> 00:58:35.269
mature, could be dramatic, could be adult,
could be a perfect medium for a story like

00:58:35.269 --> 00:58:39.699
The Lord of the Rings,
was absolutely true, and Bakshi’s work wouldn’t

00:58:39.699 --> 00:58:44.650
be relegated to mere novelty status.
Despite the waning attendance to his own films,

00:58:44.650 --> 00:58:49.130
his work in the 70s more or less set the tone
for feature animation in the 80s,

00:58:49.130 --> 00:58:53.589
which was dominated by the dark, often sombre
films of Don Bluth.

00:58:53.589 --> 00:58:57.799
Because everything is connected, Rankin/Bass
worked with Peter Beagle and turned his book

00:58:57.799 --> 00:59:02.539
The Last Unicorn into a haunting and mature
film in 1982.

00:59:02.539 --> 00:59:07.130
Even Disney, on the verge of bankruptcy, would
try to play to the trend with their own adaptation

00:59:07.130 --> 00:59:11.420
of a midcentury fantasy epic with the notorious
flop The Black Cauldron.

00:59:11.420 --> 00:59:15.529
Then, of course, The Simpsons would begin
airing in 1989,

00:59:15.529 --> 00:59:21.509
in 1993 MTV began a late night block of adult
animation that ran the whole gamut from crass

00:59:21.509 --> 00:59:25.839
to cerebral,
and over the course of the 80s and 90s anime

00:59:25.839 --> 00:59:30.759
would go from being a niche import to a staple
pillar of modern animation.

00:59:30.759 --> 00:59:35.539
So, that’s the story of Bakshi and the Ring.
I think what I find compelling about his Lord

00:59:35.539 --> 00:59:40.140
of the Rings is a summary of what I find compelling
about the man himself and his career as a

00:59:40.140 --> 00:59:43.959
whole,
one that is deeply flawed but undeniably bold

00:59:43.959 --> 00:59:46.450
and occasionally brilliant.

